            <ArticleTitle>Continuous lumbar sympathetic block.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>230-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 74-year-old woman with peripheral vascular disease suffered from rest pain in the right big toe and intermittent claudication. Because of concomitant venous congestion, a chemical lumbar sympathectomy was considered to carry an increased risk of leg edema. A continuous lumbar sympathetic block with local anesthetic abolished the pain in the toe without side effects. After this reversible block, a chemical lumbar sympathectomy was performed producing pain relief for 4 weeks when the patient was last seen.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
